In this problem, you'll implement a type of hash table called a leapfrog transaction table that's a hybrid between a chained hash table and a linear probing hash table. Leapfrog hashing, like linear probing and Robin Hood hashing, is an open-addressing table, which means the hash table is made of a collection of individual slots, each of which can either be empty or hold a transaction record. The slot type is as follows:

    struct Slot {
        std::string transactionID;
        SlotType type; // SlotType::EMPTY or SlotType::FILLED
        int link;
    };

This slot type is the same as what you'd find in standard linear probing with one major change: the link field. This field can hold one of two values: either the constant NOT_LINKED, indicating that the slot is not linked to another slot, or the index of another slot in the table.

We can visualize a leapfrog transaction table by drawing it out like a regular linear probing table, with the addition of links between slots. For example, here's a sample leapfrog transaction table. Here, we're using a (bad, just for expository purposes) hash function that hashes each transaction ID to its last digit:

In this picture, if there's a link from one slot to another, we've drawn an arrow from the first slot to the slot it links to. Empty slots, and slots whose link is NOT_LINKED, are drawn with no outgoing arrows.

Lookups in a leapfrog transaction table work as follows. As in linear probing or Robin Hood hashing, we begin by hashing the transaction ID in question to find its home slot. If that slot is empty, then the transaction is definitely not in the table and we can stop looking. If that slot is full and contains the transaction ID we're looking up, great! We've found it. Otherwise, the transaction might still be in the table, but just not in the position it wants to be in, so we will need to search for it.

This is where the link field comes into play. Rather than using the linear probing technique, we'll instead move from this slot to the slot given by the link field, seeing if the transaction ID is there. We'll keep following the links from one slot to the next until either (1) we find the transaction ID or (2) we need to follow a link pointer from a slot whose link field is the constant NOT_LINKED, indicating that the slot has no outgoing link. (This behavior of jumping around the table is where the name "leapfrog hashing” comes from.)

For example, suppose we want to look up transaction ID 1234 in the above table. We begin by hashing 1234 to get slot 4, which is our starting slot. That slot is full, but contains a transaction ID other than 1234 (specifically, 5678). We therefore follow its link to slot 6. Slot 6 has 2345 in it, which isn't what we're looking for, so we follow its link to slot 8. Slot 8 contains 1234, so we've found the transaction ID we're looking for.

As another example, suppose we're looking for transaction ID 9999, which is not in the table. We begin by hashing 9999 to get slot 9, which currently holds 8888. That's not what we're looking for. Since slot 9 has no outgoing link, we stop searching and report that 9999 is not in the table.

To look up transaction ID 5678, we hash to slot 8, find that it is present, and therefore signal that 5678 is in the table.

Next, suppose we want to look up transaction ID 4567. We begin by jumping to slot 7, which holds 2345 (not what we’re looking for), so we follow the link to slot 8. Slot 8 contains 1234, which isn’t what we’re looking for, and since it has no outgoing link we stop searching and report that the table doesn't contain 4567.

Finally, suppose we want to look up transaction ID 1111. We jump to slot 1, find that it's empty, and therefore immediately know that 1111 isn't in the table.

Insertions in leapfrog hashing work as follows. We begin by first checking that the transaction ID we want to insert isn't in the table; if it is, then we don't need to do anything. Otherwise, the transaction ID isn't present, and we need to add it. We jump to the slot given by the transaction ID's hash code, then follow the links until we come to the last slot in the chain. For example, suppose we want to insert transaction ID 6789 into this table:

Transaction ID 6789 has hash code 9, so we begin in slot 9. We follow links from slot 9 to slot 1, stopping in slot 1 because there is no outgoing link.

At this point, we need to find another free spot in the table at which to insert 6789. To do so, we use a regular linear probing search starting in the slot just after the one we ended in (wrapping around, of course) until we find a free slot. Our chain ended in slot 1, so we start a linear probing search from slot 2. That slot is empty, so we place 6789 there, linking slot 1 to slot 2. Since 6789 is now at the end of its chain, we set its link to NOT_LINKED, since nothing comes after it.

Now, suppose we insert transaction ID 2345. We jump to slot 5 to start, then follow links until we reach the end of the chain. That takes us to slot 6. From here, we do linear probing, starting in slot 7, to find a free slot. That wraps us around the table to slot 1. We put 2345 there, adding a link from slot 6 to slot 1.

You might have noticed that all items in a chain have the same hash code (1234, 2345, 3456, and 4567; 5678, 6789, and 7890). But that's just a coincidence and isn't guaranteed to be true. For example, suppose we insert transaction ID 1111. We jump to slot 1, then follow the link to slot 2, then use linear probing to place 1111 into slot 3.

To recap, here's how the lookup and insertion procedures work:

• Lookup: Jump to the slot given by the transaction ID's hash code, and stop if the slot is empty. Otherwise, follow links forward until you either find the transaction ID or run out of links to follow.

• Insert: Proceed as with a lookup. If you don't find the transaction ID, use linear probing starting from right after the last slot visited until an empty slot is found, placing the transaction ID there and updating links as appropriate.

There are some edge cases to watch out for when dealing with insertions. You have to make sure not to insert the same transaction ID twice, not to insert into a full table, and to handle the case where the initial slot is empty.

